<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:luna="clr-namespace:EmuSen.LunaP.Controls">

    <!-- The grid's definitions are built in code, because how many tracks there
         are and which way round they go depends on Orientation and on whether
         either pane is null. What stays here is the look.

         A WIDE GRAB AREA WITH A THIN LINE IN IT, which is two requirements that
         disagree: the divider should read as a hairline and be catchable with a
         mouse. Fluent's GridSplitter paints its whole width, so a 4pt grab area
         would be a 4pt bar. So the splitter is transparent and the rule is a
         separate one-pixel Border in the same grid cell, sized and turned by
         the same code that already decides which way the grid runs.

         PART_Rule is not hit-testable, and that is load-bearing rather than
         tidy: it sits ON TOP of the splitter in the same cell, so if it took
         input it would swallow the middle of the drag target and the divider
         would only be draggable on the pixels either side of the line. -->
    <Style Selector="luna|SplitPane">
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <ControlTemplate>
                <Grid Name="PART_Grid">
                    <ContentPresenter Name="PART_First" />
                    <!-- NAMED, because Avalonia's GridSplitter is a tab stop that
                         responds to the arrow keys - measured: one Left press
                         moves a 200pt pane to 190. It is a control a keyboard
                         reaches and uses, so an unnamed one is a place the focus
                         can land with nothing to say what it is. §26.11. -->
                    <GridSplitter Name="PART_Splitter"
                                  Background="Transparent"
                                  AutomationProperties.Name="{TemplateBinding DividerLabel}" />
                    <Border Name="PART_Rule"
                            IsHitTestVisible="False" />
                    <ContentPresenter Name="PART_Second" />
                </Grid>
            </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- SET FROM A STYLE, NOT AS AN ATTRIBUTE ON THE BORDER ABOVE, and the
         difference is the whole reason `split-pane .rule` works at all.

         A value written into a ControlTemplate lands at BindingPriority.Template,
         which OUTRANKS Style - measured, §39.2: with the background on the Border
         element, a `split-pane .rule { background: … }` rule parsed, matched the
         part, and lost, leaving PART_Rule at #ff6e6e6e with priority Template. The
         vocabulary promised a themeable divider and quietly could not deliver one.

         As a style setter it is Style priority, so a host theme applied afterwards
         wins in the ordinary way. The rendered default is identical; what changes
         is who is allowed to override it. -->
    <Style Selector="luna|SplitPane /template/ Border#PART_Rule">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource LunaBorder}" />
    </Style>

</Styles>
